Ladybird saves a force-dark choice per host, next to the global
default, and WebContent decides each top-level document’s value as
that document becomes active — so the first paint is already right.
Same-process child frames follow the top-level document.

Chromium does the same only on Android: It stores a per-site
AUTO_DARK_WEB_CONTENT content setting, scoped to the top-level origin,
through WebContentsDarkModeController’s isEnabledForUrl() and
setEnabledForUrl(). Then after each navigation, its
ChromeContentBrowserClient::OverrideWebPreferencesAfterNavigation()
resets WebPreferences::force_dark_mode_enabled from
WebContentsThemeClient::IsForceDarkWebContentEnabled(). Ladybird
differs in three ways: It keys the choice by host, as its per-site
zoom does; it keeps every explicit choice, even one that matches the
default; and it decides inside WebContent rather than in the browser
process after the navigation. Gecko and WebKit have no built-in
force-dark mode, so they have no per-site choice to compare.
